---
import ComparisonPageLayout from '../../layouts/ComparisonPageLayout.astro';

// The OpenCode claims changed in this refresh were checked against
// opencode.ai docs and the anomalyco/opencode repo on 2026-10-11 (permissions
// page re-checked 2026-10-13); sources are in the verification block below.
// This page covers opencode.ai, not the archived opencode-ai/opencode project
// (now Charm's Crush).
const comparison = {
  competitor: {
    name: 'OpenCode',
    icon: '💻',
  },
  tldr: {
    title: 'TL;DR',
    points: [
      'Both are open source: OpenCode is MIT, Caro is AGPL-3.0. Both can run local models.',
      'OpenCode is a general-purpose coding agent with 75+ model providers. Caro is a shell specialist.',
      'By default OpenCode allows most operations, shell commands included, without asking. Exceptions: doom-loop and outside-directory actions ask, and .env reads are denied. You can turn on prompts and write wildcard rules. Caro checks every command against 52+ curated patterns with risk levels, with no configuration.',
      'OpenCode runs offline once you configure a local provider and turn off its model, update and LSP downloads. With its default embedded backend, Caro is offline after a one-time model download.',
      'They work well together: OpenCode for coding, Caro for quick, safety-checked shell commands.'
    ]
  },
  headToHead: [
    { feature: 'Open source', caro: true, competitor: true, winner: 'tie' },
    { feature: 'Offline by default', caro: true, competitor: 'partial', winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Asks before running shell commands inside the project, by default', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Built-in dangerous-command patterns', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Risk level for each command', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Custom command rules (patterns, allow/deny lists)', caro: true, competitor: true, winner: 'tie' },
    { feature: 'Shell command specialist', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Multi-provider model support', caro: true, competitor: true, winner: 'tie' },
    { feature: 'General code editing and refactoring', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
    { feature: 'MCP servers and custom agents', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      title: 'Scope and Focus',
      icon: '▸',
      content: `
        <p><strong>OpenCode</strong> is a general-purpose AI coding agent. It edits files, refactors code, explains codebases, runs terminal commands, uses MCP servers and supports 75+ model providers. It has a terminal UI, a desktop app and IDE extensions.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> does one job: help you write and safely run shell commands. That focus allows deeper shell knowledge, such as platform detection and GNU/BSD awareness, and stronger safety defaults.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Safety Approach',
      icon: '■',
      content: `
        <p><strong>OpenCode</strong> allows most operations by default, including shell commands. Its documented exceptions: doom-loop and outside-directory actions ask, and reading <code>.env</code> files is denied (<code>.env.example</code> is allowed). You can set <code>bash</code> to <code>ask</code> and write wildcard rules such as <code>"rm *": "deny"</code> in <code>opencode.json</code>. The built-in Plan agent asks before edits and shell commands. A plugin can check or block any command before it runs. OpenCode does not ship a curated list of dangerous commands.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> checks every command against 52+ curated patterns and rates it Safe, Moderate, High or Critical. At the default safety level it blocks Critical commands, such as <code>rm -rf /</code> and fork bombs, with no configuration, and asks before High ones. You can add your own patterns and allowlist entries.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Local Operation',
      icon: '$',
      content: `
        <p><strong>OpenCode</strong> works with Ollama, LM Studio and llama.cpp. By default it still goes online to fetch model metadata, check for updates and download language servers. Each of these has an off switch.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> is offline-first with its default embedded backend. After a one-time model download it never needs the internet, which suits air-gapped networks, flights and restricted VPNs.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Use Both',
      icon: '▸',
      content: `
        <p>Use <strong>OpenCode</strong> for coding work: editing files, explaining code and refactoring. Reach for <strong>Caro</strong> when you want one shell command, checked for safety, in seconds.</p>
      `
    },
  ],
  verdict: {
    chooseCaro: [
      'You need specialized shell command help',
      'You want safety checks on by default, with no configuration',
      'You want a lightweight, focused tool',
      'Offline or air-gapped use is a requirement',
      'You are adding to your tools, not replacing them',
    ],
    chooseCompetitor: [
      'You need a general-purpose AI coding agent',
      'File editing and refactoring are your main tasks',
      'You want one tool for many coding tasks',
      'You want a choice of 75+ model providers',
    ]
  },
  verification: {
    date: '2026-10-11',
    sources: [
      { label: 'anomalyco/opencode repository (MIT)', url: 'https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode' },
      { label: 'OpenCode docs: permissions (re-checked Oct 13, 2026)', url: 'https://opencode.ai/docs/permissions/' },
      { label: 'OpenCode docs: config', url: 'https://opencode.ai/docs/config/' },
      { label: 'OpenCode docs: providers', url: 'https://opencode.ai/docs/providers/' },
      { label: 'OpenCode docs: plugins', url: 'https://opencode.ai/docs/plugins/' },
    ]
  }
};
---

<ComparisonPageLayout
  title="Caro vs OpenCode - Feature Comparison | Shell AI Tools"
  description="Compare Caro and OpenCode. A shell specialist with safety checks on by default vs an open-source general-purpose AI coding agent."
  comparison={comparison}
  heroSubtitle="Specialized shell companion vs general coding agent"
  finalCtaHeading="Specialized. Safe. Focused."
  finalCtaSubtext="Add Caro to your toolkit for shell command expertise with guardrails."
  verdictSubtitle="Or use both—they complement each other well!"
/>
